\pard\sa200\sl276\slmult1\f0\fs22\lang9\lquote India was where the British faced the greatest challenge from nationalism in the years 1918-1939.\rquote Assess the validity of this view\par
\par
India \f1\endash  nationalism a challenge\f0\lang2057 :-\f1\lang9\par
\bullet  Charismatic leadership \endash  Gandhi, Nehru, Jinnah etc.\par
\bullet  Mass movement \endash  esp after Amritsar. No longer just a movement confined to educated elites.(For example, in the United Provinces (UP), Congress had nearly 1.5 million members by 1938,over 90% of whom were from rural areas).\par
\bullet  Tactics of civil disobedience \endash  the British could not respond using coercion in the changed moralclimate after WWI and Amritsar: \lquote 1919 was a turning point in the history of India and Amritsar wasthe pivot\rquote  \endash  James. Gandhi\rquote s tactics: civil disobedience, satyagraha, the salt march (1930) etc.were difficult for the British to combat if they wanted to retain the moral high ground.\par
\bullet  Global shift in opinion after WWI: Wilson and \lquote national self-determination\rquote  \endash  the concept oftrusteeship meant that empire was more suspect than before. Nationalists were working with thegrain of changing global opinion.\par
\bullet  Significant concessions were made: The Montagu declaration in 1917, Montagu-Chelmsfordreforms, 1919 (establishing a dyarchy)/ Government of India Act, 1935 (establishing a federalsystem, Indians now self-governing at provincial level, and widening the franchise) \endash  unlike before1914, the British were forced to engage with the nationalist movement and made substantialsteps towards self-government.\par
\bullet  Why a potential threat to the whole empire? India was of such economic and strategicimportance: Curzon: \lquote As long as we rule India we are the greatest power in the world. If we lose it,we shall drop straightaway to a third-rate power.\rquote  Yet, by 1930s independence was clearly on theagenda and a conceivable possibility.\par
\par
India \endash  nationalism less of a challenge\f0\lang2057 :-\f1\lang9\par
\bullet  Serious divisions within the nationalist movement \endash  between the INC and the Muslim League.Also, divisions between Gandhi and Nehru on visions for India. Gandhi found it difficult to gatherall Indian opinion behind him, and there was a chronic difference in interests between the elitemembers of Congress and the mass of people they represented: \lquote though the imperial regimeappeared less the natural government endowed with izzat, prestige, no obvious alternative wasaccepted by all Indians.\rquote  (J Brown, p. 315)\par
\bullet  Nationalism not really apparent within the Princely States \endash  collaboration with the traditionalelites (who largely supported British rule) remained an important pillar of the Raj.\par
\bullet  The concessions/ reforms made by the British can be seen as purely cosmetic \endash  they ceded littlereal power on matters of importance. Sept 1939, the Viceroy declared war on Germany withoutconsultation and, during the war, the British arrested and incarcerated leading nationalists.\par
\bullet  Ultimately, Britain retained a monopoly of force through the police and, most importantly, theBritish and Indian armies, the latter of which remained broadly loyal as demonstrated in WWII. In1939 few British foresaw that India would be independent within ten years (Neville Chamberlain,Prime Minister, envisaged it might come in the 1980s).\par
\par
The Middle East \endash  nationalism a challenge\f0\lang2057 :-\f1\lang9\par
\bullet  Egypt: Rising nationalism during and after WWI: the Wafd and Said Zaghul + widespread unrest in1919 leading to 8 weeks of fighting. British had made promises of self-government during WWIand, in 1922, granted nominal independence, with a further treaty in 1936 granting fullindependence.\par
\bullet  Iraq and Jordan: TE Lawrence had helped unleash Arab nationalism during WWI, and theMcMahon correspondence had promised self-government. Yet, the Sykes-Picot agreement gaveIraq and Jordan as mandates to Britain \f2\u8594?\f0  unrest in 1919 and 1920 in Iraq (high costs ofmaintaining order). Faisal I appointed king in 1922. Independence granted in 1932.\par
\f1\bullet  Palestine: competing nationalisms of Jews and Arabs as Jewish migration increased into 1930s.Arab Revolt from 1936 (20,000 British troops sent to deal with it) + resistance from Jewish groupssuch as the Haganah. British unable to come up with any coherent plan to deal with Palestine.\par
\bullet  Why a potential threat to the whole empire? The Middle East was the link that kept trade anddefence within the empire together (through Suez \endash  \f0\'be of shipping in 1920s was British).Therefore, it was of vital strategic importance, especially in the run up to WWII. Also, the discovery of oil reserves meant that it had huge potential economic value. Continuingimportance of Egypt\rquote s cotton for British industry.\par
\par
The Middle East \f1\endash  nationalism less of a challenge\f0\lang2057 :-\f1\par
\bullet  It could be said that the British managed to protect all their key interests in the region, despite the\f0\lang2057  \f1 concessions made:\par
\bullet  Egypt: arguably the concessions of autonomy were purely cosmetic. The 1936 treaty left Britain incontrol of the Suez Canal, and they maintained garrisons and RAF bases in Egypt, as well as thenaval facilities at Alexandria. The test came in 1942 when the British launched a coup,overthrowing King Faruq who was suspected of sympathy for the Axis, and installing Mustafa al-Naha as Prime Minister who supported the Allies. Egypt, therefore, remained an important basefor the British when they needed it in WWII.\par
\bullet  Iraq: even after 1932 independence, the British still dominated the oil industry and retained RAF\f0\lang2057  \f1 bases in the country. Similarly to Egypt, in 1941 the British were able to restore their power in Iraq\f0\lang2057  \f1 after the government sought help from the Axis powers.\par
\bullet  Palestine: competing nationalisms meant that Britain\rquote s role was to balance them \endash  it was notclear what the independent state would be nor whether it would be viable.\par
\bullet  Lawrence James: \lquote The coup de main against Iraq [in 1941] and the palace coup in Cairo nine\f0\lang2057  \f1 months later were proof that, despite over twenty years of nationalist ferment, British power in\f0\lang2057  \f1 the Middle East was still firm.\rquote\par
\par
Ireland \endash  nationalism a challenge\f0\lang2057 :-\f1\par
\bullet  Irish nationalism was long-established and ably led by Eamon de Valera who refused to\f0\lang2057  \f1 compromise with the British.\par
\bullet  The election in 1918 of a Sinn Fein majority and the declaration of an independent Irish Republicled to the Anglo-Irish War which the British could not win. Ireland is a clear example of a violent,\f0\lang2057  \f1 guerrilla nationalist campaign, forcing the end of British rule: the Anglo-Irish Treaty left Ireland\f0\lang2057  \f1 partitioned.\par
\bullet  It was clear that, after WWI, the British were not willing to wage an unlimited military campaign to\f0\lang2057  \f1 keep Ireland within the UK.\par
\bullet  Although the south formally had dominion status, British interests were not protected as Irish did\f0\lang2057  \f1 not allow the treaty ports to be used during WWII.\par
\bullet  Why a potential threat to the whole empire? Ireland was an integral part of the UK and, as Joseph\f0\lang2057  \f1 Chamberlain had predicted, if Britain couldn\rquote t hold on to it, then the rest of the empire looked\f0\lang2057  \f1 vulnerable. Irish nationalism inspired nationalists elsewhere, and their actions proved that the\f0\lang2057  \f1 British could be defeated using concerted military action \endash  Britain was not willing to containnationalism indefinitely.\par
\par
Ireland \endash  nationalism less of a challenge\f0\lang2057 :-\f1\par
\bullet  Even the Irish nationalists were forced to compromise with the British in the Anglo-Irish Treaty: it\f0\lang2057  \f1 remained a dominion with the monarch as Head of State, and the northern six counties remained\f0\lang2057  \f1 part of the UK.\par
\bullet  Ireland, although close to Britain, never had the strategic or economic significance of other parts\f0\lang2057  \f1 of the empire such as India or the Middle East. Put simply: the British could afford to let it go.\par
\par
Sub-Saharan Africa \endash  nationalism not really a significant force\f0\lang2057 :-\f1\par
\bullet  Nationalism much slower to form in Africa: artificial boundaries created by colonial powers +much smaller educated, urbanised elites than other parts of the empire. Therefore, Britaincontinue to rely on collaboration with indigenous hierarchies.\par
\bullet  But, some evidence of nationalism starting to appear: 1919 National Congress of West Africa\f0\lang2057  \f1 founded in Accra; 1925 West African Students\rquote  Union founded; Kikuyu resistance to whitesettlers in Kenya in the 1920s.\par
\par
Conclusion: you could reasonably make a case for any of the following, as long as your essay is well-argued:\par
\bullet  You might conclude that India was where the British faced the greatest challenge fromnationalism and that this was a serious threat to the empire compared to nationalism in otherplaces.\par
\bullet  Alternatively, you might conclude that nationalism in India was manageable compared tonationalism in other parts of the empire where it was more of a challenge.\par
\bullet  You could judge that it was because nationalism existed in so many places around the\f0\lang2057  \f1 empirethat made it so threatening.\par
\bullet  Or, you might consider that it wasn\rquote t threatening anywhere and that, for the British Empire in the\f0\lang2057  \f1 inter-war period, the problems of nationalism were easily contained\f0\lang9\par
